Now update the CSS:

- Add `left`/`top` to the rail-chip transition so they glide when recentering.
- Replace `.rail-chip.unaffordable` with `.rail-chip.hide{ display:none; }`.
- Add return-in pop animation.

Also, since the chips are closer together, the shadow pseudo-element `::before` z-index -1 might clip? Fine.

Note: `.rail-chip` currently has `transition:transform ..., opacity .2s, filter .2s;`. Add `left .26s ..., top .26s ...`.

The `return-in` animation on the inner `.chip`:

```css
.rail-chip.return-in .chip{ animation:chipReturn .34s cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.3,1.35) both; }
@keyframes chipReturn{
  0%{ transform:scale(.35) translateY(18%); opacity:0; }
  55%{ transform:scale(1.12) translateY(-6%); opacity:1; }
  100%{ transform:scale(1) translateY(0); opacity:1; }
}
```

But wait, `.rail-chip.locked` filter stays while return animation runs; okay.

Potential conflict: `.chip` transform animation ends with `both` → leaves transform scale(1) translateY(0), which creates a stacking context but fine. Better to not use `both` so the transform resets after the animation (avoid creating stacking contexts). Use no fill or `forwards`? The end state is identity, so no fill needed. Without fill, it snaps back to no transform at the end anyway. Use no fill.

Also add `@keyframes` near the other keyframes.

Should hidden chips `display:none` be applied also when `locked`? Yes.

